#
# prima-materia — the searches of the run on Tzava'at ha-Rivash, 2026-10-15
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:HasidismSakanaSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Every caution, danger, bodily harm and named failure in Tzava'at ha-Rivash"@en, "Jede Warnung, Gefahr, Körperschädigung und benannte Misslingensform in Tzava'at ha-Rivash"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Tzava'at ha-Rivash, sections 1 to 143, read complete in the Hebrew of the Warsaw printing of 1913; every passage found checked in the translations of Jacob Immanuel Schochet (1998) and of Amiram and Yehudah Shimon Markel (2020)."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any statement that a practice taught in the work can harm the practitioner's body or soul, endanger him, or fail to bring about what it is practised for, and any account of why it failed."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Hebrew: סכנה, ימות, למות, מיתה, יחלה, חולה, יחליש, חלש, יתקלקלו, פגם, עצבות, מרה שחורה, בכיה, לא עלתה בידם, לריק יגע, אין לו שום תקנה, לא יקובל, נפסק ממנו כל החיות, יפול מהדביקות, קרי, עבירה בלילה, לכלבא אתמסר, בנידוי; in the English of both translations at the same places: danger, die, death, ill, weaken, damage, blemish, sadness, melancholy, weeping, not succeed, in vain, no remedy, not accepted, cut off, fall, nocturnal, dogs, ban."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HasidismHitbodedutYechidiSakana, pmc:HasidismTefilaMitaBaTeva, pmc:HasidismTehillimMachlishGuf, pmc:HasidismHistaklutKeri, pmc:HasidismTaanitEinayim, pmc:HasidismAtzvut, pmc:HasidismDiburRaHaChayut, pmc:HasidismHistaklutBaPanim, pmc:HasidismKlipaRochevet, pmc:HasidismSigufimLeMadrega, pmc:HasidismLaRikYaga, pmc:HasidismTefilaAlHaChisaron, pmc:HasidismGelidMashal, pmc:HasidismTefilaPresupposesBriut, pmc:HasidismHastaraPresupposesMadrega ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-15" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-15" ;
    skos:note "Nine cautions and four failure conditions, the mortification for a level among them although it is typed as a caution; the fifth failure condition, walking with God by chance in section 31, stands in the note of pmc:HasidismDevekutRuachHakodesh. Two prerequisites state their consequence. Six of the cautions name a consequence of the body: danger in seclusion alone, death by nature from the intention of prayer, weakness from psalms before prayer, nocturnal emission from looking for desire, harm to the eyes from fasting, and the life force cut off by bad speech. Not entered as a caution: section 26, that one who does not rise at midnight without compulsion is under a ban above, because the consequence is a status above and the practice is a rule of the calendar; and section 116, the punishment after death of one who stops to talk on the way to a commandment, because the consequence is posthumous and narrated from other books."@en .

pmp:HasidismSimanSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether Tzava'at ha-Rivash gives a sign by which the practitioner or another can tell that attachment or right service has come"@en, "Ob Tzava'at ha-Rivash ein Zeichen nennt, an dem der Übende oder ein anderer erkennt, dass das Anhaften oder der rechte Dienst eingetreten ist"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same work in the same witnesses, the Hebrew read complete."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any sign, mark, criterion or perceptible change named as showing that attachment, fervour, prayer with intention or proper service has occurred, and any statement that it cannot be told from outside."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Hebrew: סימן, וסימנך, ניכר, יראה לאדם אחר, נראה, מאריך, במהירות, מעצמם, נקלה, תמיד, פעם עובד ופעם אינו; in the English at the same places: sign, mnemonic, recognise, appears to another, prolong, rapidly, by themselves, lowly, always, sometimes serves."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:HasidismSimanimShelDevekut, pmc:HasidismGelidMashal, pmc:HasidismShmiraYetera ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-15" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-15" ;
    skos:note "Three signs, one counter-sign and one criterion. The word siman occurs mostly as a mnemonic, vesimanekh, introducing a verse (1, 16, 17, 18, 19) and not as a sign of a state; only section 114 uses it for a sign of service. The counter-sign is section 105, the best prayer looking to another like prayer without attachment. The criterion is the ice of section 115. The parable of the guarded king turns the absence of the expected state into a sign of nearness."@en .

pmp:HasidismZmanSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether Tzava'at ha-Rivash names a term, a count of attempts or a condition under which a practice is to be given up"@en, "Ob Tzava'at ha-Rivash eine Frist, eine Zahl von Versuchen oder eine Bedingung nennt, unter der eine Übung aufzugeben ist"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same work in the same witnesses, the Hebrew read complete."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A term in days or years after which a promised effect is to be expected, a number of repetitions after which nothing more is to be expected, or an instruction to stop a practice because it has not worked."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Hebrew: ימים, שנים, פעמים, ג' ימים, משבת לשבת, ששה פעמים, יפסיק, יניח, יבטל, לא יאמין שהיום; in the English at the same places: days, years, times, three days, Sabbath to Sabbath, six times, stop, cease, give up, do not believe that today."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-15" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-15" ;
    skos:note "Nothing. The work counts in three places — six times the nations in the Warsaw reading of section 14, the first three days of a fast in section 43, a fast from Sabbath to Sabbath in section 77 — and none of the counts is a term after which an effect falls due. It contains the opposite of a stop rule twice: section 72, do not believe that today you certainly will not pray; and section 56, one who desires to fast is not to cancel it although joy without mortifications is better. Section 61 says what to do when attachment fails half way through a prayer — go on with small intention to the end — which is a rule for continuing, not for stopping."@en .
